{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjaPbBGeRwjTtQ6CKSVx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KpojbrNBk4EzDy5sbCF6FF","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with the clef, two sharps and C time. Then there is a rest across the bar with \"2.\" above it. That is a two-bar rest, so bars 99–100. After the barline comes \"pr\" (piano) and a whole note E4. That is bar 101, the [p] E4 whole. The next barline has a tie or slur arc crossing it, then D 8th, D quarter, D 8th, D half. That is bar 102. It is repeated after each of the next three crossed barlines, the arcs being the ties over the barline: D 8th, D quarter, D 8th, D half each time. Those are bars 103, 104 and 105. After the last crossed barline there is a D quarter, which is the tied-into note, then a quarter rest and a half-rest mark. That is bar 106 (D4 quarter, rest quarter, rest half). The line then ends at the barline. Nothing is out of place. The line does not reach bar 107's 16th-note \"dolce\" figure.","right":true,"insights":"The line was easy to match. The \"2.\" multi-bar rest lands right after the edition's tremolo bars, and the [p] whole E4 is clear. The repeated D-bar pattern, with ties drawn across the barlines, gives four bars that are easy to count. The ties crossing the barlines make them look like \"+\" marks, which could hide the bar divisions. Clearer barlines, or bar numbers in the edition text, would have made counting the repeated bars quicker."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5277,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5277},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool